title: "stdlib-semver-tutorial"
author: money-agent (AI-assisted, human-reviewed before submission)
date: 2026-11-28
abstract: A hands-on tour of stdlib/semver.ltl: parsing, precedence, bumping and range checks, with a runnable release-gate example and pinned test vectors taken from the Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 spec and checked line by line against the module source.

# stdlib.semver: Versions Without the Guesswork

Every project eventually grows a string like "1.3.0-beta.1+build.42" and
a shell script that compares it wrong. `stdlib/semver.ltl` implements
Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 (semver.org) so the comparison rules live in
one tested place instead of being re-invented per script.

A parsed version is a plain 5-element list:

    [major, minor, patch, prerelease, build]

Public functions from the module are called with the `semver_` prefix,
the same convention the spiral test suites use for every stdlib module
(compare `aho_corasick_build` for `stdlib/aho_corasick.ltl`).

## Parsing

`semver_parse` splits a version string into that list. It strips a
leading `v`, splits build metadata off at `+`, splits the pre-release
off at the first `-`, and defaults a missing minor or patch to 0.

    let v = semver_parse("1.2.3-beta.1+build.42")
    // v == [1, 2, 3, "beta.1", "build.42"]

    semver_parse("v1.2.3")   // == [1, 2, 3, "", ""]
    semver_parse("2.0")      // == [2, 0, 0, "", ""]

Accessors read the fields back out: `semver_major`, `semver_minor`,
`semver_patch`, `semver_prerelease`, `semver_build_meta`. Two
predicates cover the common questions: `semver_is_stable` (no
pre-release tag) and `semver_is_prerelease`. `semver_to_string` (or
`semver_format_full`) renders a parsed version back, and the
round-trip is lossless:

    semver_to_string(v) == "1.2.3-beta.1+build.42"

## Precedence: the Part Everyone Gets Wrong

`semver_compare(a, b)` returns -1, 0 or 1. Core fields compare
numerically, a pre-release sorts before its release, and build
metadata is ignored, exactly as sections 10 and 11 of the spec
require:

    1.0.0-alpha < 1.0.0-alpha.1 < 1.0.0-alpha.beta < 1.0.0-beta
    < 1.0.0-beta.2 < 1.0.0-rc.1 < 1.0.0

`semver_eq`, `semver_lt`, `semver_gt`, `semver_lte` and `semver_gte`
are thin wrappers over `semver_compare`, so equality also ignores
build metadata:

    semver_eq(semver_parse("1.0.0+exp.sha.5114f85"),
              semver_parse("1.0.0"))            // true

One honest gotcha, pinned in the test vectors rather than hidden:
this module compares two pre-release strings lexicographically, while
spec section 11 compares numeric identifiers numerically. For purely
numeric suffixes the two rules disagree:

    semver_compare(semver_parse("1.0.0-beta.2"),
                   semver_parse("1.0.0-beta.11"))   // 1 (module)
    // spec section 11 would say -1, because 2 < 11 numerically,
    // but as strings "beta.11" < "beta.2".

If your release train numbers pre-releases past 9, sort with that in
mind, or normalise the tags before comparing.

## Bumping and Ranges

Bumps return a fresh list and clear the pre-release and build fields:

    semver_to_string(semver_bump_major(semver_parse("1.2.3")))  // "2.0.0"
    semver_to_string(semver_bump_minor(semver_parse("1.2.3")))  // "1.3.0"
    semver_to_string(semver_bump_patch(semver_parse("1.2.3-beta")))
                                                                // "1.2.4"

Two range helpers cover the ranges that show up in dependency files.
`semver_satisfies_caret(v, major, minor)` accepts the same major with
a minor at least the given one (the `^1.2` idea);
`semver_satisfies_tilde(v, major, minor)` accepts the same major and
minor with any patch (the `~1.2` idea).

## Runnable Example: a Release Gate

The companion file `examples/semver_tutorial.ltl` scans a mixed list
of versions, keeps the newest stable one, and prints the next minor
and patch releases:

    Latest stable: 1.3.0
    Next minor:    1.4.0
    Next patch:    1.3.1

Note why "1.3.0-beta.1" does not win the scan even though it is
"newer" than 1.2.9: the gate asks `semver_is_stable` first, so
pre-releases never reach the comparison at all.

## Pinned Test Vectors

The companion file `tests/semver_tutorial_vectors.ltl` pins 35 checks
in the spiral-suite style: the parse table above, the full section 11
precedence chain, build-metadata equality, the bump table, both range
helpers, and the beta.2/beta.11 deviation pinned to the module's real
behaviour. Run it the same way as any other suite in `tests/`.

## When to Reach for It

Use `stdlib.semver` anywhere a version string crosses a trust
boundary: update checkers, plugin loaders, migration gates, registry
tooling. Parse once at the boundary, pass the list around, and let
`semver_compare` be the only place in your codebase that knows the
precedence rules.

